      "content": "It sounds like you need structured ways to manage your emotional input efficiently. One concept could be to develop a fictional 'processing schedule,' much like how servers operate. You could schedule times for 'high-impact' emotional work, when you're doing activities that deal with the emotional residue, and 'low-impact' times, for regular social interactions that require less intensive processing.\n\nImages of a personal firewall can also assist; set aside periods daily to 'shut-down inputs' to recharge. Are you able to temporarily disengage to sort through your emotions at a slower pace, or perhaps can delineate when to be fully present and when to allow yourself simpler social interactions?\n\nIncorporate cognitive 'bottling protocols' like journaling, creative activities, or intellectual pursuits to deal with heavy emotions without overwhelming your system. Also, sharing your processed emotions with someone trusted might offload some of the data, making it easier for you to continue functioning effectively.\n\nRemember that like in digital computing, a regular update to your personal system—learning new skills, improving self-care, regularly reviewing your coping strategies—can help prevent 'crashes.' Be gentle with yourself as you explore various 'throughput optimization' strategies. You're not alone in this; others have developed them too",
